es due to lockdown  measures  have all contributed to a sharp decline  in children getting crucial vaccinations  experts warn that a further loosening of  pandemic restrictions  could lead to multiple outbreaks of  preventable illnesses  if countries are continuing to loosen  their public health and social measures  in spite of what we're saying  about covid that we're not past the  we're not past  the worst yet and vaccines are  continuing to roll out for covid so now  is not the time to release  public health and social measures but if  that's happening and as it's happening  we are going to see more and more  transmission  of the pathogens that are otherwise  vaccine preventable pathogens  the world health organization says  action is urgently needed  to ensure that the covid19 pandemic does  not deflect from the need to protect  children  from other deadly and avoidable diseases  and dr kate o'brien who we just heard in  that report  joins us now she's director of  immunization vaccines and biologicals at  the wh oh good to have you with us  so when we talk about uh 23 million  children  missing out on basic vaccines in 2020  due to the pandemic are we talking about  children primarily in developing in  poorer regions  or is this a problem across the globe  you know these um these children who  have missed out on vaccines are really  seen in all countries around the world  but they are predominantly found in some  of the regions  uh more than others so in particular in  the africa region  is one of the the areas that is  particularly hard hit as well as the  south  east asia region but i want to emphasize  that this is not just a problem of  four countries you know on the list of  the top 10 countries  that have unimmunized children  we find countries like brazil the  philippines and mexico  and this is partly attributable to the  size of the country  big countries have more opportunity for  unimmunized children  but also the missing out of vaccines and  a slippage in the uh in the program  coverage  and what consequences has uh this had so  far do we know  well in part for some of the diseases  that our  children are vaccinated against it's bit  too early to tell what the impact will  be  there are very considerable efforts  being made to try to catch these  children  up to try to find them again and give  them the vaccines that they missed  in the course of 2020. but we're also  starting to see  measles outbreaks in some countries and  measles as you know  is one of the most transmissible viruses  and one of the viruses that really can  harm a child's health even if they  survive the illness for years to come  afterwards  and how much you you say we're sort of  trying maybe to to catch up how much  leverage is there in terms of timing if  health services everywhere were to pull  their sleeves up and  and start doing routine vaccinations for  kids again can we catch up  we certainly can catch up and there are  a number of different ways that um  country programs catch up first of all  making sure that parents know  how important it is to bring their kids  back or initiate  vaccination even for those kids who who  didn't get the vaccines at the point  when they were you know the very  earliest  age as little babies the second is that  there are campaigns that can take place  we can strategize in a different way so  that that  vaccines can be given you know through a  special program  where parents and the whole community is  informed about the importance of  of coming to get kids vaccinated  especially those  who have missed out so we do have lots  of ways that the catch-up can happen  and what we saw in 2020 is that things  were really  pretty bad in march and april programs  really  fell backwards in terms of what they  were able to do but many countries made  a very special effort  and caught up a lot in the second half  of the year and especially  in october november december we have  seen a slip in the coverage  it wa